Symfony UX 3.7 is out, and it ships UX CSS: an experimental package that gives Twig a `css()` function inspired by [Panda CSS](https://panda-css.com/). The [release announcement](https://symfony.com/blog/symfony-ux-3-7-released) covers what it does. This article is about how it works inside, and how I built it.

UX CSS makes two promises. For the same configuration, it gives the same class names and the same CSS as Panda, without running Panda or Node.js. And a `css()` call written in the template costs nothing at runtime. Neither one holds by accident.

Here is the second one in action. This template:

{% verbatim %}
```twig
<button class="{{ css({ p: 'md', color: 'primary', _hover: { color: 'fg' } }) }}">Save</button>
```
{% endverbatim %}

compiles to this PHP:

```php
yield "<button class=\"";
yield (string) $this->escaper->escape(("p_md c_primary hover:c_fg"), "html", null, true);
yield "\">Save</button>";
```

The class names are already there, as a plain string. Let's see how they got there, and how we know they are the right ones.

## Panda's test suite is the spec

When you port a library from another language, writing the code is not the hard part. Knowing when you're done is. No document says which class name Panda gives to `{ width: ['50px', null, '60px'] }`, or in which order it writes the rules. Panda's behavior is the spec, and its own test suite is the best description of it.

So UX CSS doesn't rewrite Panda's tests: it records them. The [`tools/panda-golden/record.mjs`](https://github.com/symfony/ux-css/tree/3.x/tools/panda-golden/record.mjs) script:

1. clones Panda at a pinned commit;
2. patches Panda's source to add recording hooks, in the rule processor, the parser test fixture, the token and reset generators, and the static CSS helpers;
3. runs Panda's vitest suite, then five codegen scenarios from Panda's sandbox, where it wraps the generated `css()` function;
4. writes every recorded call, with its input, its config and its output, as JSON fixtures.

The patches go through a small helper that refuses to guess:

```js
const replaceOnce = (file, search, replacement) => {
    const content = readFileSync(file, 'utf8');
    const first = content.indexOf(search);
    if (first === -1 || content.indexOf(search, first + 1) !== -1) {
        throw new Error(`Expected exactly one occurrence of ${JSON.stringify(search)} in ${file}.`);
    }
    writeFileSync(file, content.slice(0, first) + replacement + content.slice(first + search.length));
};
```

If Panda changes the code where a hook goes, the script stops and prints the snippet it couldn't find, instead of silently recording fewer cases. Moving to a newer Panda means changing the commit and running the script again (about a minute once Panda's dependencies are installed). Then you fix what broke.

Here is one recorded case, from Panda's `atomic-rule.test.ts` (trimmed):

```json
{
    "inputs": [{ "width": ["50px", "60px"] }],
    "expected": {
        "classNames": ["w_50px", "sm\\:w_60px"],
        "css": "@layer utilities {\n  .w_50px {\n    width: 50px;\n}\n\n  @media screen and (min-width: 40rem) {\n    .sm\\:w_60px {\n      width: 60px;\n}\n}\n}"
    }
}
```

The recorder produces 522 cases like this one, from 41 of Panda's test files. It also records what `utility.transform()` returns for each of the 455 utilities, with a few values each: 4,388 more records. Add the token and validation tests, and the golden suite runs **5,108 PHPUnit tests** built from Panda's behavior.

Not every case is in scope. 156 were flagged as unsupported when they were recorded (JSX, `cva()`, patterns, config hooks), and 53 more are out of scope for this first version: the template literal syntax, merging several style objects in one call, themes, hashed class names, and a few others. Each of the remaining 313 cases gives the same output as Panda, byte for byte.

Those 313 are listed in [`expectations.php`](https://github.com/symfony/ux-css/tree/3.x/tests/Fixtures/Panda/expectations.php), and that list is a ratchet. A case on it must keep passing, and the script that updates the list refuses to drop one unless you pass `--allow-regressions`. Supporting a new Panda feature means making its cases pass. Then they are locked in.

Validation follows the same idea. When a call in Panda's tests sits under a `@ts-expect-error` comment, the recorder notes it. [`PandaValidationTest`](https://github.com/symfony/ux-css/tree/3.x/tests/Golden/PandaValidationTest.php) then checks that the PHP validator rejects what Panda's TypeScript types reject, and accepts the rest.

## Byte for byte, really

Matching Panda byte for byte forces you to port things nobody would port by choice.

JavaScript and PHP disagree on small things. An object in JavaScript lists its integer-like keys first, in ascending order, while a PHP array keeps the insertion order. `\s` doesn't match the same characters in JavaScript and in PCRE. Numbers don't turn into strings the same way. So [`JsValue`](https://github.com/symfony/ux-css/tree/3.x/src/Engine/JsValue.php) reimplements JavaScript's conversions, "so that values come out exactly as in Panda":

```php
// JavaScript's \s; PCRE's \s differs on U+0085 and U+FEFF
public const WHITESPACE = '[\t\n\x{0B}\f\r \x{A0}\x{1680}\x{2000}-\x{200A}\x{2028}\x{2029}\x{202F}\x{205F}\x{3000}\x{FEFF}]';
```

The CSS goes the same way. Panda runs its output through postcss, and the order of the passes shows in the result. [`Optimizer`](https://github.com/symfony/ux-css/tree/3.x/src/Engine/Css/Optimizer.php) runs the same passes on a small hand-written CSS tree, in the same order, down to a merged rule keeping the whitespace it had before the merge. [`MergeRules`](https://github.com/symfony/ux-css/tree/3.x/src/Engine/Css/MergeRules.php) ports the copy of postcss-merge-rules that Panda inlines. There is no PostCSS and no Node.js involved.

Only one comparison is normalized: token CSS, because Panda parses it several times and leaves random indentation behind. Everything else must match exactly.

## Twig does the work at compile time

Back to the first promise. Twig compiles each template once into a PHP class, and before that, a node visitor can rewrite the template's tree. [`CssNodeVisitor`](https://github.com/symfony/ux-css/tree/3.x/src/Twig/CssNodeVisitor.php) finds every `css()` call and resolves as much as it can while the template compiles.

A word on vocabulary first: in Twig, `{ p: 'md' }` is called a *hash*. Nothing gets hashed here: class names stay readable (`p_md`), and Panda's `hash: true` option is not supported.

A hash of constants becomes one string, as shown at the top. The visitor also handles a few common shapes:

- a ternary with constant branches becomes two strings, and Twig picks one when the page renders;
- a variable set once to a constant with {% verbatim %}`{% set %}`{% endverbatim %}, before the call, in the same body, counts as a constant;
- a mixed hash is split: the constant part is compiled to a string, and only the rest goes to the runtime.

For a ternary, {% verbatim %}`{{ css({ p: 'md', color: active ? 'primary' : 'fg' }) }}`{% endverbatim %}, the compiled template holds both class lists (variable lookup simplified):

```php
("p_md")." ".($active ? ("c_primary") : ("c_fg"))
```

For a mixed hash, {% verbatim %}`{{ css({ p: 'md', _hover: { color: color } }) }}`{% endverbatim %}, only the dynamic key reaches the runtime:

```php
$this->env->getRuntime("Symfony\\UX\\Css\\Twig\\CssRuntime")->append(("p_md"), ["_hover" => ["color" => $color]])
```

The visitor is careful with variables. A variable only counts if it is set exactly once, before the call, and not inside an `if`, a `for` or a `with`. The tests list nine cases that must stay at runtime, like a variable set twice, or set after the call. When in doubt, the runtime takes over: a wrong constant would be a silent bug, while a runtime call costs 1 to 10 µs.

One more detail: even a constant string still goes through Twig's escaper. That's on purpose. [`CssExpression`](https://github.com/symfony/ux-css/tree/3.x/src/Twig/CssExpression.php) is not a constant expression, so Twig escapes it like any other string. The cost is the cost of printing a string.

Since the visitor validates the hash while compiling, a mistake becomes a Twig syntax error, with the template and the line:

```
Unknown property "colr". Did you mean "color" in "error.html.twig" at line 1?
```

So `lint:twig` catches it in CI, and `cache:warmup` fails at deploy, outside of debug mode.

## Where the stylesheet comes from

Class names are half of the job. The other half is the CSS file.

The node visitor accepts a collector. [`TemplateScanner`](https://github.com/symfony/ux-css/tree/3.x/src/Dumper/TemplateScanner.php) parses every template whose source contains `css(`, without rendering it, and every hash the visitor resolves lands in an index, per template, with its modification time. [`StylesheetDumper`](https://github.com/symfony/ux-css/tree/3.x/src/Dumper/StylesheetDumper.php) then merges all of them, writes one rule per style however many templates use it, and saves `var/ux_css/styles.css`.

In dev, a request listener scans only the templates that changed since the last request, and rewrites the CSS only if its content changed. With 200 templates, that's about 2 ms per request when nothing changed, and about 15 ms after one template changed. In prod, a cache warmer writes compact CSS: about 180 ms for 200 templates. AssetMapper serves the file from `var/ux_css`, and Webpack Encore or Symfony Reprise import it like any other stylesheet.

A scan can't find a value only known when the page renders. For those, the `static_css` option lists the values to generate ahead of time, and in debug mode, the runtime logs a warning when a class has no CSS.

## Teaching editors and PHPStan about `css()`

This part happened outside of the Symfony UX repository. If you write bundles, it's the one you can reuse.

A `css()` call in Twig is a hash literal, and no tool knew its keys. So in debug mode, UX CSS writes `config/reference_css.php`: a `CssStyles` array shape built from your tokens, conditions and breakpoints, which [`CssRuntime::css()`](https://github.com/symfony/ux-css/tree/3.x/src/Twig/CssRuntime.php) takes as its parameter type:

```php
/**
 * @psalm-type ColorsToken = 'blue.500'|'primary'|string
 * @psalm-type CssStyles = array{
 *     color?: ColorsToken|array<ColorsToken|array<array-key, mixed>>,
 *     p?: SpacingToken|array<SpacingToken|array<array-key, mixed>>,
 *     _hover?: CssStylesNested,
 *     md?: CssStylesNested,
 *     ...
 * }
 */
```

That raised two problems.

First, no editor completed the hash passed to a Twig function from the array shape of its PHP parameter. I sent [a pull request](https://github.com/Haehnchen/idea-php-symfony2-plugin/pull/2889) to the Symfony plugin for PhpStorm, released in version 2026.2.314, and [Symfony Language Tools](https://github.com/symfony/language-tools) got the same feature in 0.24.0. It works for any Twig function with an array shape parameter, not only `css()`.

Second, PHPStan. The shape has about 840 keys, 126 of them pointing to a nested shape, and analysing a file with a single `css()` call **took 66 seconds** with PHPStan 2.2.16. I opened [an issue](https://github.com/phpstan/phpstan/issues/15348) with a public reproducer. [Markus Staab](https://github.com/staabm) picked it up, and a series of fixes, including one of mine, landed in PHPStan 2.3.0 and 2.3.1. The same analysis **now takes 3.6 seconds**.

When I opened the PhpStorm pull request, I wrote that UX CSS "may not be released" because of these performance issues. A few upstream fixes later, it shipped. The nesting is still typed one level deep only (`CssStylesNested`), because PHPStan refuses a type that refers to itself, and each nested level slows the analysis down.

So if your Twig function takes a hash, an array shape on its PHP parameter now buys you completion in both editors. And when a tool chokes on your types, a small public reproducer is the fastest way to get it fixed.

## What's next

UX CSS is experimental. Some Panda features are not supported yet: Panda's recipes (`cva()`) and slot recipes, patterns (`stack`, `grid`...), text, layer and animation styles, themes, and hashed class names. Each of them is a set of skipped golden cases waiting for their turn, and supporting one means making those cases pass.

Two honest notes:

1. "Same as Panda" is exactly as true as the tests: for the syntax in scope, against one pinned Panda commit. Outside of that, it's a promise I have not checked yet.
2. About 64,000 of the 114,000 lines of [the first pull request](https://github.com/symfony/ux/pull/3933) are recorded fixtures. Reviewers could skip them, but they are the part of the code I trust the most.

The [documentation](https://symfony.com/bundles/ux-css/current/index.html) has everything else, and there is [a live demo](https://ux.symfony.com/css) on ux.symfony.com. Try it on a page of yours, and if something doesn't fit your application, [open an issue](https://github.com/symfony/ux/issues). Now is when feedback matters most. 🚀
